# Dev Server Port Configuration

## Rule

This project **only runs on specific ports**. When running `npm run dev`, the Vite dev server must be accessed on:

- **localhost:3000** — Primary development port
- **Official production/preview URL** — When deployed (e.g., `arsplatform.onrender.com`)

## Why

The application is configured with hardcoded or environment-specific API base URLs and CORS policies that only allow these origins. Other ports will cause:

- **CORS errors** when calling backend APIs
- **Authentication failures** because the allowed origins don't include the port
- **Environment variable mismatches** if VITE_API_BASE_URL is scoped to specific domains

## What NOT to Do

```bash
# ❌ WRONG — These ports will NOT work
npm run dev  # (if it starts on 5173)
npm run dev -- --port 3001
npm run dev -- --port 5173
npm run dev -- --port 8080

# The app may load visually but API calls will fail
```

## How to Run Correctly

```bash
# ✅ CORRECT — Always use port 3000
npm run dev  # Starts on localhost:3000 by default

# If Vite starts on a different port, kill and restart with:
npm run dev -- --port 3000
```

## Port 3000 Specifics

The project is configured in `vite.config.ts` to use port 3000 as the default dev server port. Do not change this without also updating:

- CORS configuration in the backend
- Environment variables (`VITE_API_BASE_URL`)
- Any allowed-origin policies on the server

## Remember

> When testing or debugging, always ensure you're accessing the app on **localhost:3000**, not any other port. If you see CORS errors or 401/403 responses, first verify you're on the correct port.
